[cot:thought] US high-propensity applications total 4.38 per employer formation. The national range
[cot:thought] spans 2.61 to 7.45 with a median of 4.06. Twenty-eight states exceed four applications per employer. The 22.8% BF8Q-to-HBA conversion sits below the typical 27% HBA-specific rate.
[cot:thought] This gap likely reflects pandemic cohort effects, seasonal adjustment mismatches, or revised definitions.
[cot:thought] Seasonal-adjustment
[cot:thought] differences between HBA and BF8Q are a minor concern for long-run ratios. The BF8Q-to-HBA ratio may overstate true HBA conversion because some employer formations originate from non-HBA applicants.
[cot:thought] The exact relationship is BF/HBA = HBA_conversion + NHBA_conversion ?? (NHBA/HBA).
[cot:thought] The headline will
[cot:thought] read roughly 4.4 high-propensity applications per eventual employer, or about 23% formation-to-HBA proxy conversion. Historical HBA denominators were restated after January 2026, which would raise conversion rates rather than lower them. The post will include honest caveats. Next step is generating the data time-series file from the aggregated JSON.
